WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.270
Film Courage: What are your first steps in
the screenwriting process?

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:07.580
Jack Perez, Filmmaker: I think the first steps
are when I begin to write are a lot of thinking

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.510
about it.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:14.900
Like I’m not really good at just sitting
down and beginning to bang it out.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.710
And oftentimes I don’t write it out.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:23.080
It’s not like I have to make an outline
first and then make the script.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.720
So for me it’s usually a couple of ideas
that just won’t go away.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.629
If those ideas won’t go away then that means
that hopefully there is something there and

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:41.539
I just let it percolate sort of magnetic and
if it’s a good idea it’s sort of rare,

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:46.230
I’ve never been one of those guys who was
able to spin a good yarn and have a million

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.760
stories I want to tell.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.160
But in the case of SEARCH &amp; RESCUE a couple
of ideas started to bind together.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:01.620
And I think some of them were ideas that I
had for other movies that didn’t go anywhere.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:07.300
Now that I think about it they were several
different ideas and characters that I had

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.050
been developing for other movies that didn’t
sustain themselves.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:17.650
They were good but for some reason they didn’t
belong in whatever movie I was trying to make

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:23.140
and so it all started to combine into this
one film.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:32.100
But that’s specific to this particular movie
and every writing process is different.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.500
If someone comes to me and says “I need
you to write MEGA SHARK VERUS GIANT OCTOPUS”

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.450
that’s a different process…okay Act 1:
this stuff should happen and then this…that’s

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:46.850
when I do that.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.820
So work-for-hire, I think that’s easier.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:56.740
A simpler genre is easier to do then this.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.690
But I really did have to think about it.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:04.260
I really had to draw from a lot of ideas that
had been dormant that all sort of coalesce

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.820
to form this thing.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.979
That’s how it started and then I had to
sit down and write everyday, like everybody

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:11.980
else you had to write it.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.110
You can think about it all day long but you
have to write it.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.270
Film Courage: Did you have dedicated times
to write?

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:17.780
Jack: I did.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.840
I kept talking about it and finally my wife
finally said “You need to write this thing.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.110
You just need to do it.”

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:29.590
And certainly without Valerie’s insisting
that I just get down to it, I probably wouldn’t

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:30.590
have done it.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:36.840
But I did, I just basically realized that
if I got up at five in the morning everyday

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.390
that I would be in this…you know a lot of
writers talk about this kind of haze where

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.710
you’re not really judging, you’re not
in a critical mind, you’re just in a semi

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:46.710
dream state.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:51.991
That if I got up at five and wrote until nine
or something like that, if I wrote for like

00:02:51.991 --> 00:02:57.310
three or four hours in the morning that I
could bang out without me judging myself I

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:05.540
would just knock out four pages or five if
it was a good day over however long it was

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.700
(a month) just every day I just knocked out
a little bit.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:17.450
At least if you do the math after five pages
a day times 20 days is what…a hundred pages?

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.150
So within shy of a month you can get a draft.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.270
And then you go back and you’re like “Oh,
this is bad and this doesn’t work.”

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:32.500
And you are actually able to tackle it critically
but at least the hard part of getting it out

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.470
was accomplished if I was diligent and got
up and just did it.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:40.020
And that’s hard, it’s like going to the
gym or whatever, that takes…anybody will

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.060
tell you that.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:46.360
And some days you don’t have anything, you
just sit there and you’ve got one line.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.910
But I think as all real writers will tell
you that’s necessary.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.390
The writer writes.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:56.300
You can’t expect to just not do it and then
have great stuff pour out.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.430
You have to go to the gym.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.080
So that’s how it happened.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.051
Film Courage: You said something earlier that
you have a friend who is a “real writer

00:04:06.051 --> 00:04:07.051
writer.”

00:04:07.051 --> 00:04:10.670
And I was just wondering how is that different.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.780
Jack: I think a real writer writer is someone
who just writes all the time.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.090
That’s all they do.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.329
It’s not even about physically making the
movie, it’s about writing.

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.980
They write one script and they start working
on the next script.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.050
Or they write one novel and they are already
outlining the next novel.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.979
They just what I consider dyed-in-the-wool
writers.

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:34.669
They are not hyphenate writer/directors, they
are just writers.

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:38.570
And I have real respect for that because it’s
very myopic.

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:44.419
Because it’s like this is their skill and
it’s a very difficult thing to be a good

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:45.419
writer.

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:46.740
So I have a lot of respect for that.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:53.289
I don’t think I’m a writer naturally,
I’m a writer out of necessity.

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:59.090
In other words, I became a writer because
I couldn’t expect screenplays to fall out

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:01.349
of the sky for me to direct.

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:07.029
So I became a writer so I could have some
control over the films I wanted to make.

00:05:07.029 --> 00:05:12.639
But I feel like it was a longer process to
become a pretty good writer.

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:15.080
Whereas being a director felt more natural
to me.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.659
I just had a more natural instinct for it.

00:05:17.659 --> 00:05:20.599
Whereas writing felt like work.

00:05:20.599 --> 00:05:23.789
Writing still feels like work to a certain
extent.

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:28.509
Whereas when I’m making a film even though
it’s hard, it never feels like work.

00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:32.270
It just feels natural for some reason.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:37.620
I feel like writers that are the real writers
are the ones that they speak that language,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.460
they hear that music.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.289
That’s their primary way of expressing themselves.

